<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">&mdash; the seam, at the scale of a charge</p>
    <h2 id="t-seam-small">The Smallest Seam Anyone Has Measured</h2>
    <p>This chapter has argued that one seam recurs at every scale: the horizon of a black hole, the
      threshold at one, the moment of passing. The claim is cheap unless the seam shows up somewhere small
      enough to be instrumented. It does, and it has a name that has nothing to do with this framework:
      confinement.</p>
    <p>Colour charge is never observed alone. What reaches a detector is always a colour bound to its own
      inversion &mdash; a quark with an antiquark, or three quarks taking one of each &mdash; and pulling
      the pair apart does not separate them; past about a femtometre the stored energy makes a fresh pair
      instead. The two faces cannot be prised off one another, which is exactly the property that
      distinguishes a one-sided surface from a two-sided one.
      <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="lede">A horizon is a seam you can fall through. Confinement is a seam you cannot get behind.
      The framework reads them as the same join at two scales, and the argument is laid out properly at
      <a href="/moebius/#t-colour">the seam and the colour charge</a>.</p>
    <p class="muted">The caution that belongs with it: QCD explains confinement with a running coupling and
      a flux tube, quantitatively, on a lattice, to several significant figures. The framework explains it
      with a topology and no number. Where a complete account already exists, a geometric restatement is a
      reading, not a rival, and it is logged as one.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">A word with several jobs</p>
    <h2 id="t-seam-primer">What Counts as a Seam, and What Does Not</h2>
    <p>In ordinary language, a seam joins two edges while remaining a special place in the finished object.
      Mathematics makes that idea precise by identifying boundary points according to a rule. Physics uses
      several related but non-identical ideas: a material interface, a phase boundary, a causal horizon and a
      topological identification can all separate regimes, yet they obey different equations.</p>
    <p>TTI uses “seam” as a family name for transitions where a description cannot simply continue unchanged.
      At a black-hole horizon, the issue is causal access. At a cell membrane, it is regulated transport. In
      confinement, it is the impossibility of isolating colour charge. At the experienced present, it is the
      update from remembered state to current state. Similar language can organise comparison, but it does not
      make these mechanisms physically identical.</p>
    <p>A seam is therefore not any line drawn on a diagram. It must come with a rule answering three questions:
      what can cross, what changes while crossing, and what remains invariant. Without those answers, “seam”
      is a metaphor. With them, it may become a boundary condition, transition map or measurable interface.</p>
    <p class="lede">The shared pattern is restricted passage, not shared substance. Each scale must earn its
      own crossing law.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">Four cases in one comparison</p>
    <h2 id="t-four-seams">Do Not Let the Analogy Erase the Differences</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="grid two">
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>A horizon</h3>
      <p>An event horizon is a causal boundary defined by spacetime geometry. An infalling observer need not
        strike a material surface there, yet signals from inside cannot reach a distant exterior observer.
        The horizon is observer-sensitive and governed by relativity, not by a membrane&rsquo;s chemistry.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>A living membrane</h3>
      <p>A cell membrane is matter. Channels, pumps and receptors selectively move substances and information,
        consuming energy to maintain differences. It is crossed continually. Its “inside” and “outside” are
        biological compartments with measurable concentrations.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>Confinement</h3>
      <p>Quantum chromodynamics does not place quarks behind a tiny wall. The interaction changes with scale,
        and attempts to separate colour charges produce bound hadrons rather than isolated quarks. Topological
        language may suggest a picture, while QCD supplies the quantitative account.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>The present moment</h3>
      <p>The “now” is not known to be a thin physical surface sweeping through the universe. Relativity denies
        a single universal present. TTI can model updating records as a seam, but must not turn subjective
        succession into an absolute cosmic slice without further law.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">From a boundary to a direction</p>
    <h2 id="t-crossing-arrow">Why Crossing Alone Does Not Derive Time&rsquo;s Arrow</h2>
    <p>A transition map may be reversible. If every output uniquely determines its input, applying the inverse
      reconstructs the earlier state. An arrow appears only when the process loses accessible information,
      couples to an environment, selects special boundary conditions or becomes overwhelmingly likely in one
      direction. The presence of a seam does not by itself provide any of these.</p>
    <p>Thermodynamics gives a statistical account. For a chosen coarse-graining, vastly more microscopic
      arrangements correspond to equilibrium-like macrostates than to specially ordered ones. Given a low-
      entropy past, ordinary evolution is therefore likely to move toward larger macroscopic entropy. TTI&rsquo;s
      claim that smallward and largeward infinities tilt time must ultimately connect to that state counting,
      not merely restate the direction in geometric words.</p>
    <p>The temporal-antipode proposal adds boundary structure: the two infinities are assigned to opposite
      asymptotic ends and are denied a global shared clock. That can explain why the framework draws time with
      two different limits. It does not yet explain why eggs break, heat flows or memories refer to the past.
      Those are empirical asymmetries requiring dynamics and special conditions.</p>
    <p>One route toward a testable model would define a crossing variable \(J\), a seam map \(M\), and an
      observable loss \(L\):</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      J_{\rm out}=M(J_{\rm in}),
      \qquad
      L=H(J_{\rm in})-H(J_{\rm out}\mid E),
    \]</p>
    <p>where \(E\) records the environment and \(H\) is a stated information measure. The notation is only a
      template, but it shows what prose must eventually supply: states, transformation, record and measurable
      irreversibility.</p>
    <p>Continuity across a seam also needs care. A coordinate description may become singular at a horizon
      even when an infalling observer experiences nothing locally singular. A concentration may jump across a
      membrane while chemical potentials and flux laws remain well defined. A topological gluing may identify
      two boundary points while reversing orientation. “Something changes at the seam” is therefore too vague;
      the model must name which quantities are continuous, which jump, and which cease to be observable.</p>
    <p>This suggests a reusable reading method for the rest of the project. For every claimed seam, list the
      state on each side, write the crossing or identification rule, and track conserved quantities. Then ask
      whether the rule is reversible and whether an observer can detect the boundary locally or only through
      a global journey. Those questions turn an evocative image into a candidate model and reveal when two
      examples only share a word.</p>
    <p class="muted">Thus the page&rsquo;s strongest conclusion is comparative. Horizons, membranes,
      confinement and moments each mark restricted transitions. Their resemblance motivates questions across
      scale; their different mechanisms prevent one case from serving as evidence for the others.
      <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>
