<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--creed">
    <p class="kicker">Chapter 31</p>
    <h2 id="t-arrow">Where the Arrow Comes From</h2>
    <p>The asymmetry noticed on the very first page &mdash; that the interval below one is denser than
      everything above it &mdash; is cashed in here. If there is more room in the direction of the small,
      then the natural drift of anything free to move is downhill toward the denser infinity. Time's
      direction is the slope of that tilt. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <h3 id="t-temporal-antipodes">The stronger temporal-antipode reading</h3>
    <p>The new conjecture places the largeward and smallward infinities at opposite asymptotic ends of time.
      If neither endpoint is physically identified with the other, neither can participate in a finite
      round-trip clock comparison. Their projections may align at isolated moments, but complete phase locking
      over an interval would require an additional transport law. In that conditional sense, the two
      infinities can never fully synchronise. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p>This sharpens the arrow but does not derive it. Opposite endpoints do not explain entropy, and a
      compactified time diagram does not turn either infinity into an observable clock. The full
      <a href="/temporal-antipodes/">Temporal Antipodes conjecture</a> defines those debts; the
      <a href="/stress-tests/#t-temporal-antipodes">stress test</a> gives the claim permission to fail.</p>
    <p class="muted">The obvious objection is that thermodynamics already explains the arrow by counting
      states, and does so quantitatively. The framework's reply &mdash; that the counting itself inherits
      its asymmetry from the geometry &mdash; is taken up properly in
      <a href="/stress-tests/#t-entropy">the entropy objection</a>, where it is also conceded that this
      is the framework's weakest joint.</p>
    <p class="muted small">Next: <a href="/stress-tests/">Part IV &mdash; Tests and Essays: Stress Tests</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>
